WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.274
TypeScript gives developers a much better way to describe application data, but it does not make outside data safe by itself.

2
00:00:08.274 --> 00:00:18.125
A request body, a cookie, a message from a browser tab, or a record loaded from storage can still contain values that do not match the type you expected.

3
00:00:18.125 --> 00:00:27.818
The compiler can help you reason about your own code, but it cannot force a remote client or a third-party service to send honest, well-formed data.

4
00:00:27.818 --> 00:00:33.807
The key detail is that TypeScript types are erased when the code is compiled to JavaScript.

5
00:00:33.807 --> 00:00:41.135
Interfaces, type aliases, and annotations guide the compiler and the editor, but they are not runtime checks.

6
00:00:41.135 --> 00:00:49.646
When the application is actually running, a value that was declared as a User, Order, or Settings object is just JavaScript data.

7
00:00:49.646 --> 00:00:58.078
If that value came from outside the trusted codebase, the application needs explicit runtime validation before relying on it.

8
00:00:58.078 --> 00:01:03.200
External input shows up in more places than teams usually list during design.

9
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:04.776
Browsers submit forms.

10
00:01:04.776 --> 00:01:05.801
APIs send JSON.

11
00:01:05.801 --> 00:01:07.140
Files are uploaded.

12
00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:08.874
Queues deliver messages.

13
00:01:08.874 --> 00:01:11.869
Environment variables configure services.

14
00:01:11.869 --> 00:01:17.385
Local storage, session storage, and cookies can be changed by users or extensions.

15
00:01:17.385 --> 00:01:21.640
Partner SDKs may return unexpected shapes when versions drift.

16
00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:30.702
Each of those sources is a boundary where the program should move carefully from untrusted runtime values into typed application logic.

17
00:01:30.702 --> 00:01:35.588
A healthy TypeScript validation strategy treats validation as a bridge.

18
00:01:35.588 --> 00:01:42.444
On one side of the bridge, data is unknown because it came from a source the application does not control.

19
00:01:42.444 --> 00:01:49.064
On the other side, the program has domain objects that are safe enough to use for business decisions.

20
00:01:49.064 --> 00:01:55.919
Validation, parsing, normalization, and clear error handling are what make that bridge trustworthy.

21
00:01:55.919 --> 00:02:03.721
Without that step, TypeScript can create a false sense of confidence around data the runtime never actually checked.
